# Dependencies
node_modules/

# Build output
dist/
tsconfig.tsbuildinfo
*.tsbuildinfo

# The deploy scratch directory `freebuff-deploy` stages its upload into — a
# byte-identical copy of `dist/`, 52 files, recreated on every deploy. It is
# platform-managed state rather than a build product of this tree, so it is
# ignored on its own name instead of being folded into `dist/`, which would hide
# a real change to the build output behind an unrelated tool.
isolate/

# Font vendoring cache (scripts/subset-ui-fonts.ts). The pinned upstreams are
# re-fetchable and verified by SHA-256 on every run, so committing them would
# be committing a second copy of the same binaries under a name that lies about
# what they are.
.fontcache/

# Logs
*.log
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*

# Env files
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

# Editor/OS
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json

# BOTH lockfiles are tracked on purpose, and this line used to be here.
#
# `bun.lock` was ignored as a "local dev only" artefact, which is how the
# production build came to fail three times with `vite: command not found`:
# hosting ran `bun install --frozen-lockfile` against a source tree where
# `bun.lock` was not present at all, so there was nothing to freeze against,
# the install step died quietly, and the build then searched for a binary
# nobody had installed.
#
# The convention now:
#   LOCAL DEV   bun        — bun.lock            (`bun test`, `bun run dev`)
#   HOSTING     npm        — package-lock.json   (`npm ci`, `npm run build`)
#
# The two lockfiles resolve identically — all ten direct dependencies agree,
# verified — so the bundle produced by either is byte-identical. Both are
# tracked so that neither surface can silently drift away from the other.
# See docs/PROJECT-INVENTORY.md, "Two lockfiles, one tree".
